#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151885 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "f0520e86-8df9-5090-8d5c-761a4ba20583" :question/math-academy-id "e-6565" :question/problem "Determine which of the following second-order ODEs are homogeneous, and which are inhomogeneous.\n\n1. $\\, (2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0$\n2. $\\,y'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x$\n3. $\\,y'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order linear ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x)\n$$\n\nThe equation is homogeneous if\n\n$$\nf(x) = 0\n$$\n\nfor all $x$. If it is not homogeneous, then it is inhomogeneous.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each option.\n\n- The equation\n$(2-\\sin(x)) y'' + x y' + y = 0$\nis homogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 0$ for all $x$.\n- The equation\n$y'' + 6y' + 9y = 3e^x$\nis inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 3e^x$, which is different from zero.\n- Writing the equation\n$y'' + x^3 y - 8 = 0$\nin the form $ay'' + by' + cy = f$, we have\n$y'' + x^3 y = 8$.\nThis equation is inhomogeneous since, for the right-hand side, we have $f(x) = 8$, which is different from zero."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151886 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9a926134-0870-5528-985d-2c92e71158f4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Linear ODEs"}] :question/id #uuid "45688683-2788-58bd-8cf5-80feab863551" :question/math-academy-id "e-6566" :question/problem "Which of the following second-order differential equations are linear?\n\n1. $y'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2$\n2. $y''+y'=x \\cos x$\n3. $yy'' - y'+ 9 = 0$" :question/worked-solution "A second-order linear differential equation has the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x)\n$$\n\nLet's look at each equation in turn.\n\n- The equation\n$y'' + (y-1)y'+x = 2$\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $(y-1)y'$.\n\n- The equation\n$y''+y'=x \\cos x$\nis a second-order linear ODE with $a(x) = 1, b(x) = 1, c(x) = 0$, and $f(x) = x\\cos{x}$.\n- The equation\n$yy'' - y'+ 9 = 0$\nis **NOT** linear because it contains the nonlinear term $yy''$.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151887 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "2d0a7ede-b896-56c6-9b60-a6aec8eff9e8" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Initial Value Problems"}] :question/id #uuid "1826d315-cef0-5b5f-b654-61733a63bf2f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6567" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is an initial value problem?\n\n1. $x^2y'' + xy' = x+2$\n2. $9y'' + 4y = e^x, \\quad y(0) = 1, \\quad y'(4)= 1$\n3. $y''+xy'+x^2y= \\sin x, \\quad y(1) = 0, \\quad y'(1)= 5$" :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at the *same* location in the domain of $y$, then this is an **initial value problem.**\n\nWith this in mind, let's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- System I is not an initial value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n- System II is not an initial value problem because it consists of a differential equation together with two conditions in *different* locations, $x=0$ and $x=4$. So this is a **boundary value problem**, not an initial value problem.\n- System III is an initial value problem. The differential equation is together with two conditions at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=1$).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151888 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "66ec8603-ae96-5212-88e1-4571d7face6b" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying the Associated Homogeneous Equation of a Second-Order Inhomogeneous ODE"}] :question/id #uuid "4f4f4539-466a-5cc0-9470-8b94d6221093" :question/math-academy-id "e-6568" :question/problem "For the differential equation $2xy'' +y' - y = \\cos x$, what is the associated homogeneous equation?" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have a second-order inhomogeneous ODE given in the form\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = f(x)\n$$\n\nThe associated homogeneous equation is constructed by replacing $f(x)$ with zero:\n\n$$\na(x)y'' + b(x)y' + c(x) y = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, in our case, the associated homogeneous equation is\n\n$$\n2xy'' +y' - y = 0\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152488 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "90930e15-0827-5e0b-82cf-98263fc756d6" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Second-Order Homogeneous ODEs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8b3ade63-1e70-501f-ae43-08bc563a5b96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149789} {:answer/id #uuid "cbef941c-4f70-50ba-a00f-108f81bc0da2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149790}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e873100d-c852-5d87-9cbb-248e4cc13e84" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149788} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "eebd4864-8ce9-546e-87ad-9e7a376b562a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "inhomogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149792} {:answer/id #uuid "ef368c32-84e1-5d1d-afa9-f181948d778a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149793}] :answer-field/id #uuid "e2ca1e7a-9f7c-55ce-99ca-b6bd65a1a751" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149791} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58bfac0e-9113-533e-8329-967ecc57984b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "homogeneous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186149795} {:an